  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a blood vessel–associated microenvironment regulates both normal stem cells and disseminated tumor cells as they move through different states. Across the top, normal stem cells in a vascular niche progress from quiescence to activation, then progenitor-cell proliferation, and finally differentiation into mature cells. These cells sit along an endothelial surface and basement membrane, with pericytes and extracellular matrix around them. Colored dots indicate local signals: some appear to maintain survival or tethering, some promote quiescence, and others trigger activation and proliferation.\n\nThe lower half shows a parallel sequence for disseminated tumor cells. A dormant tumor cell lies near the same vascular structure, apparently held inactive by quiescence-inducing cues. With activating signals, it becomes active and then expands into a proliferating cluster. In the larger tumor mass, additional tumor-derived factors appear in the environment, suggesting that the growing cancer modifies the niche around the vessel.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that the perivascular niche can control either normal tissue regeneration or cancer outgrowth, depending on the balance of cues that keep cells dormant, activate them, or support expansion and differentiation.",
